  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「キンセンカ」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%82%AD%E3%83%B3%E3%82%BB%E3%83%B3%E3%82%AB",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 110766417,
      "text": "トウキンセンカ とヒメキンセンカ があるが、一般にキンセンカと言えばトウキンセンカの事を指す。キク科の植物。別名はカレンデュラ。ポットマリーゴールドと呼ばれる食用キンセンカと園芸キンセンカでは葉の違いや育て方の違いがある。暖地で秋蒔きすると12月～2月頃から咲き始める。通常は3月から5月が花の最盛期で、夏には株が枯れる。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Calendula officinalis」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calendula_officinalis",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1370530450,
      "text": "Calendula officinalis, Mary's gold, common marigold, the pot marigold, Scotch marigold, or ruddles, is a flowering plant in the daisy family, Asteraceae. It is probably native to southern Europe, but its long history of cultivation makes its precise origin unknown, and it is widely naturalised. The florets are edible and the plant has historically been used as medicine."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "cultivation": {
      "ja": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「キンセンカ」の節「栽培」",
        "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%82%AD%E3%83%B3%E3%82%BB%E3%83%B3%E3%82%AB#%E6%A0%BD%E5%9F%B9",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 110766417,
        "heading": "栽培",
        "text": "種まきは秋か早春にするが、寒冷地以外は秋まきが普通である。18-25℃の気温下では種まき後5日ほどで発芽する。土は選ばないが酸性土壌では育ちが悪く、弱アルカリ性の土壌を好む。また日当たりを好む。茎は直立性で20cmから1mの高さ。よく分枝するので、切り戻してそれを促す。開花期間は春先から初夏である。栽培は容易であるが、うどんこ病、アブラムシが発生する。",
        "truncated": false
      },
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Calendula officinalis」の節「Cultivation」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calendula_officinalis#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 1370530450,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "Calendula officinalis is widely cultivated and can be grown easily in sunny locations in most kinds of soils. Although perennial, it is commonly treated as an annual, particularly in colder regions where its winter survival is poor, and in hot summer locations where it also does not survive.\nCalendulas are considered by many gardening experts as among the easiest and most versatile flowers to grow in a garden, especially because they tolerate most soils. In temperate climates, seeds are sown in spring for blooms that last throughout the summer and well into the fall. In areas of limited winter freezing, seeds are sown in autumn for winter color. Plants will wither in subtropical summer. Seeds will germinate freely in sunny or half-sunny locations, but plants do best if planted in sunny locations with rich, well-drained soil. Pot marigolds typically bloom quickly from seed (in under two months) in bright yellows, golds, and oranges.\nMost cultivars have a spicy aroma. It is recommended to deadhead (remove dying flower heads) the plants regularly to maintain even blossom production.\n\n■ Cultivars\n\nNumerous cultivars have been selected to showcase a wide range of variations, spanning from pale yellow to orange-red, and with 'double' or 'semi-double' flowerheads with ray florets replacing some or all of the disc florets. Examples include 'Alpha' (deep orange), 'Jane Harmony', 'Sun Glow' (bright yellow), 'Lemon' (pale yellow), 'Orange Prince' (orange), 'Indian Prince' (dark orange-red), 'Pink Surprise' (double, with inner florets darker than outer florets), 'Green-heart Gold' (double, bright yellow), 'Apricot Pygmy' (double light peach) and 'Chrysantha' (yellow, double). Additionally, the cultivar 'Variegata' boasts yellow variegated leaves.",
        "truncated": true
      }
    }
  },
